/**
 * VEML 3.x parser (conformance clauses A.1–A.10).
 *
 * DOMParser + hand-written mapping into the typed document model.
 * One-way parse; zero Babylon imports; main-bundle code (written
 * size-consciously — the bundle budget has little headroom).
 *
 * Wire reality (conformance A.3): the dominant real-world document declares
 * NO xmlns at all yet still uses `xsi:type` — an undeclared prefix is a fatal
 * XML namespace error in DOMParser. So we scan the raw text for the root open
 * tag and inject `xmlns:xsi` if absent. Version classification then reads the
 * DOM root's namespaceURI:
 * 3.0 and 3.1 are accepted, no namespace is accepted as the current schema
 * ('none'), and any OTHER declared namespace is a load error naming the found
 * namespace and the supported ones.
 *
 * Leniency (A.7/A.8): existing 3.x worlds were written for a .NET
 * XmlSerializer reader, which neither enforces minOccurs nor rejects unknown
 * content, so the parser matches that leniency rather than the XSD.
 * Missing elements yield absent fields; unknown elements/attributes emit
 * capability warnings and parsing continues. The warnings are additive
 * reporting: the parse result is the same as if they were silently ignored.
 *
 * Note on absent optional fields: they are assigned `undefined` rather than
 * omitted (equivalent under this tsconfig; JSON/structured-clone drops them).
 */

